ommunity  to open up in some ways marginally we  now have a really good cohort  of international correspondents that are  on the ground  as well as working with independent  journalists from cuba in  cuba on the ground reporting on this so  in this case  what we're seeing is that the the  actions of these people are being  broadcast through social media  through traditional media but also  through local media  independent journalists as i mentioned  like joanie sanchez and others are  posting this and blogging about it and  reporting it in live time  but they're also reporting on the  crackdown that we've seen there are  pictures of bloodied  one person has died a bloodied  photographer from the ap  this is going to be very very powerful  powerful because often in the past  the numbers of journalists that space  has been very limited and consequently  they've been able  basically the regime has been able to  repress with impunity  well you mentioned at the start of this  interview that many of the reasons that  protesters have taken to the streets are  that they are disappointed with what is  quite frankly  a failed uh socialist regime  and as you point out there's a lot of  international attention on  this now because the press from very  local press to the international press  is giving it quite rightly that  attention i've noticed that a lot of  voices who would often be sympathetic to  those ideas  um in the international community in  western countries are  oddly silent on this one do you think  they're going to have to pipe up and  make a comment on it at some point  you know i'm not sure about that again  lenin referred to people like that as  useful idiots for the revolution they're  sort of unwittingly enabled or complied  with uh  revolution and the denial of people's  rights um i think it's gonna be very  difficult for some people to part with  the myth and the romanticization of of  the castros  uh of uh the regime i'd like to believe  that but you know having worked on cuba  for a long time  and having uh experienced and tried to  mobilize  uh people to react to uh crackdowns in  the past for example the  this black spring of 2003 i i've just  been astounded by the double standards  and hypocrisy of  of people who are willing to denounce  human rights abuses say in chile under  pinochet  um and then just suddenly shy away from  doing so in cuba and i  i suspect that me that may be the case i  mean there are a few i think that will  start to speak out michelle bachelet  who's a  high commissioner for human rights at  the u.n has been very shy about speaking  out before on cuba in fact  it's alleged i haven't confirmed this  that she has never personally spoken out  on human rights abuses in cuba i think  it's going to be very difficult for them  to do that but i think other people a  number of academics  journalists and like artists uh are  going to be a little bit reticent to do  that  and it's a shame because whereas in the  past maybe they could try to fool  themselves  maybe when a castro was in power they  could claim that this was  you know this was the mantle of a  revolutionary leader and they're still  beholden to all those myths of the  revolution but now it's it's post castro  and the images are clear not just to the  crackdown but also the people that are  on the streets  but i honestly i hate to sound cynical i  don't have any great faith in people  that before have been silent suddenly  speaking up  yeah and i'm i'm going to be cynical  along there with you  and i'm also going to ask you a very  broad question so forgive me in advance  but i'm just so curious  about how the political attitudes are  shifting in cuba  now you know we want to avoid  stereotypes because like any other  country you're going to have a broad  spectrum  of political opinion but in relation to  these protests i mean  if they were to have some success what  would that success look like and what  are the new political demands that are  growing  in cuba and and is there a divide  between say the younger generations and  the older generations that might be  slightly more sympathetic to the regime  well it's a good question and it's  difficult as you say because  cuba's diverse it's large it's a country  of 11 million people  um i just tell a few anecdotes about  this because i think they're  illustrative  one time i was in cuba i was having  dinner uh with a group of young  uh people not necessarily activists they  were just university of havana students  that like young professionals and i  asked them you know what do you  what do you think um because by way of  context most people in cuba recognize  the failings of the regime they  recognize and they'll speak quietly and  in hush tones  about you know the need for change  but they don't really want to speak out  and i ask these young kids  what do you hope for and almost to a  person they said we hope to leave  we have no future here and that's very  difficult and what's interesting now  is suddenly is that sense of desperation  that lack of of hope  may suddenly be being challenged to the  street rather than an effort to flee  and that's positive and i think once you  build that once you build that sense of  of hope then you get what what your  article says as people start to speak  about  the lack of fear they're not as afraid  uh and i think that's very very  important  in terms of pockets of support for the  regime you know it's very difficult to  tell of course there are people in the  government  who very much support support the regime  but you never know i mean as i  when i used to teach at columbia  university i would tell my students  you know try and describe what it's like  in cuba and the conversations you have  privately with people  you know imagine working in an office  where the same boss has been in power  for 60 years  how frustrating would that be you know  that person just  personally controls everything that  happens in the office and it depends on  on his whim uh would not be a work  environment that you'd want to work in  so we don't even know within the  government how much they support this  and lastly is one other element to this  which is very different  which is there's not a caster on power  right now last year raul castro stepped  down for the presidency of the  communist party before that he stepped  down from the presidency of the country  and this is going to be important  because you know you don't have that  myth of the revolution again you don't  have  the the cadre of guerrillas guerrillas  that were around  fidel well castro empower anymore it's a  new generation and they lack  that legitimacy that sort of  revolutionary cred if you will  um and consequently what you've heard  from diaz canel miguel diaz-canel the  president  is he's going to respond in a  revolutionary manner what he's saying is  i'm no different than the castros i'm  going to respond  the same way with an iron fist and it's  a message not just to the people and  he's called them as revolutionaries to  the streets  it's also a message to those around him  saying i'm not going to give up  don't we we're not we're not going  forward this is the same old regime and  i'm abiding by our  cafe maximo our chief leader the now  deceased fidel castro  chris thanks for coming on the show  [Music]  you


